    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Whitewater-WI/Assistant-Professor--Tenure-Track----Philosophy-and-Religious-Studies_JR10014610": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Tenure-Track) - Philosophy and Religious Studies",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Assistant Professor (Tenure-Track) - Philosophy and Religious Studies Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Job Duties: Attention: The Department of Philosophy and Religious Studies in the College of Letters and Sciences at the University of Wisconsin-Whitewater seeks a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Philosophy, with specialization in Social and Political Philosophy, beginning August 2027. This is a full-time position with responsibilities for teaching undergraduate courses in Philosophy and the capstone course in the General Education program, research, and service to the university and community. Position Summary: The teaching load for this position is 4/4 (24 credits annually), with an emphasis on high-impact instructional practices, student success, research, and professional service. The successful candidate will be expected to teach World of Ideas, which is the capstone course in the General Education program; at least one course in the History of Philosophy (time period open); and other courses in philosophy in line with their areas of specialization and competence. In addition, they will be expected to contribute to the Liberal Studies program administered by the department and help advise Liberal Studies majors. The approved standards for promotion and tenure at the time of initial contract are the standards that will be used for promotion and tenure. If there are revisions to department or college standards, a faculty member can request (in writing to the department chair) to have the revised standards applied. Teaching & Instructional Expectations Course Load: Deliver 24 credits of instruction per academic year. ( UW-Whitewater Teaching Responsibilities and Workload Policy ) Office Hours for Students: Maintain at least five office hours for students each week during the academic year (departments and/or colleges may have additional requirements). These office hours for students should be consistent to provide accessible student support and academic advising/mentoring. For faculty who teach a portion or all courses online, the modality of the office hours for students should be proportionate to the modality of the courses taught unless the department or college has additional requirements. Advising/mentoring: Use Navigate for all advising/mentoring notes. Communication: Maintain professional and timely responsiveness to student, colleague, and administrator inquiries via Navigate, Canvas, email and telephone, generally within 24–48 business hours while on contract. When off contract, use the “out of office” feature in Outlook to let individuals know who to contact in your absence. Administrative Compliance: Ensure the punctual submission of course syllabi at the start of each term, ensuring they meet all University and system-wide requirements ( UWW Syllabi Reqs ).. Contribute to curriculum development. Accessibility: Select and develop instructional materials that are Title II (ADA) compliant . All digital course content, textbooks, and media must be accessible to students with disabilities to ensure an equitable learning environment. Research, Scholarship & Creative Activity Maintain an active scholarly agenda that contributes to the field of Philosophy. Disseminate research through peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, or professional exhibitions per department and college standards. Service & Engagement Participate in departmental, college, and university-level meetings and committee work. Participate in professional and public service. Contribute to program assessment and development. Engage in professional outreach and service that aligns with the University Mission Statement and the Wisconsin Idea . Participate in at least one graduation ceremony each academic year. Workplace Expectations: Please see the link below regarding the workplace expectations at UW-Whitewater. We believe that by adhering to these expectations, we develop a university community in which all students, staff and faculty feel valued and supported. UWW Workplace Expectations Key Job Responsibilities: Department: The Philosophy and Religious Studies Department administers the Philosophy minor, the World Religions minor, and the Liberal Studies major and minor. We have six full-time faculty or academic staff members and an Academic Department Associate. The College of Letters and Sciences forms the core of the University, providing students with the essential elements of a liberal education in humanities, natural sciences, and social sciences. Compensation: Well-qualified candidates can expect a minimum starting annual salary of $60,000 , commensurate with the candidate’s education, related experience, and qualifications. UW System employees receive an excellent benefit package. To learn more about the UW System’s comprehensive benefit package, review the UW System Employee Benefits Brochure . Required Qualifications: PhD in Philosophy by the time of appointment. AOS: Social and Political Philosophy. ABD candidates may apply but must have their degree completed by the contract start date in August 2027; if not, they will be hired on a one-year contract with the understanding that their degree will be completed before January 2027 in advance of their first scheduled review. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated excellence in university-level teaching, experience with Canvas, and a record of scholarly achievement. AOC: Open; however, there is a preference for candidates who can contribute to the college’s interdisciplinary programs in Women’s and Gender Studies and/or Race and Ethnic Studies. Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Ability to interact respectfully with people with diverse socioeconomic, cultural and ethnic backgrounds Willingness and ability to be an active participant in following applicable safety rules and regulations including necessary training and drills How to Apply: Only complete application packages will be considered. This includes online submission of the following documents. If submitting more than five application materials, you will need to combine files prior to uploading and label with an appropriate name. Cover letter/letter of interest Evidence of Teaching Effectiveness Curriculum Vita Names and contact information for three professional references (letters of recommendation may be solicited at a later time) Copies of unofficial graduate transcripts (Official transcripts will be required if offered the position) Contact Information: If you have questions regarding this recruitment or if you are unable to complete the application online due to a disability or system problem, please contact us at (262) 472-1024 or hr@uww.edu . For questions regarding this position, please contact: Nathan McGovern Professor of Religious Studies Search Committee Chair prsdept@uww.edu To Ensure Consideration: Applications received by October 16, 2026 , are ensured full consideration. Applications received after that date may be given consideration at the discretion of the search committee. The most qualified applicants will be invited to participate in the next step of the selection process. CAMPUS INFORMATION: UW-Whitewater is a thriving public university located in southeastern Wisconsin. It leads the way in providing world-class, affordable higher education programs to more students than neighboring regional comprehensives in the University of Wisconsin System. A collaborative team of 1,300 faculty and staff are devoted to the success of the university’s 11,500 students at its main and Rock County campuses. The team provides high-impact practices and academic programs — online and in person — from associate to doctoral levels. Recognized nationally for affordability, inclusion, and career development, UWW serves approximately one-third first-generation students and boasts strength in many areas, especially business and education. The campus and community embrace the Warhawk Family spirit, supporting the university’s arts and nationally recognized NCAA Division-III level championship athletics. As a proud institution of access, more than 10 percent of undergraduate students use the acclaimed Center for Students with Disabilities — a priority mission since the 1970s.       UW-Whitewater's campuses are located centrally near Milwaukee, Madison, and Chicago, offering easy access to the cultural and commercial opportunities of major metropolitan areas. Near both Lake Geneva and the Wisconsin Dells, the area attracts numerous visitors from across the country. Whitewater is a vibrant college community near the Kettle Moraine State Forest offering area residents seasonal outdoor enjoyment. The nearby Rock County campus is located in Janesville, a town of 60,000 located on the Rock River and known as Wisconsin’s Park Place.   ORGANIZATION INFORMATION: The University of Wisconsin-Whitewater is part of the 13-campus Universities of Wisconsin ( https://www.wisconsin.edu ).    Wisconsin Statute 19.36(7)(b) provides that applicants may indicate in writing that their identity should be kept confidential. In response to a public records request, the University will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except that the identity of the successful final candidate will be released. See Wisconsin Statute 19.36(7)(a).    Per Regent Policy Document 20-19, University of Wisconsin System Criminal Background Check Policy and Universities of Wisconsin Administrative Policy 1275 Recruitment Policies, UW-Whitewater requires criminal background checks as a contingency to employment. A criminal background check will be conducted prior to an offer of employment. All final candidates must be asked, prior to hire, whether they have been found to have engaged in, are currently under investigation for, or left employment during an active investigation in which they were accused of sexual violence or sexual harassment. When obtaining employment reference checks, these same sexual violence or sexual harassment questions must also be asked.    The University of Wisconsin-Whitewater requires that all employees be active participants in following applicable safety rules and regulations including necessary training and drills.    For UW-Whitewater Campus safety information and crime statistics/annual Security Report, see  Annual Security and Fire Safety Report if you would like a paper copy of the report please contact the UW-Whitewater Police at 262-472-4660. UW IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Whitewater-WI/Instructional-Designer---College-of-Business-and-Economics_JR10013695": {
      "title": "Instructional Designer - College of Business and Economics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-31\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Instructional Designer - College of Business and Economics Job Category: Academic Staff Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Teaching, Learning, and Technology Specialist III Job Duties: Attention: The College of Business and Economics at the University of Wisconsin-Whitewater seeks an Instructional Designer (Teaching, Learning, and Technology Specialist III, TL009). Job Details: The Instructional Designer serves as a strategic partner and expert pedagogical consultant to faculty and academic units within the College of Business and Economics (CoBE). This position applies advanced instructional design principles, learning theories, and user-experience standards to support the design, development, delivery, and continuous improvement of high-quality learning experiences across face-to-face, hyflex, and online modalities. Operating within the CoBE Tech team, the individual in this role acts as a primary pedagogical and educational technology resource, collaborating with instructional staff to implement effective assessment strategies, select appropriate instructional technologies, and ensure compliance with digital accessibility standards. This position reports directly to the Assistant Dean of Online Education & Technology at the College of Business and Economics. This is a full-time, 12-month Academic Staff position. Key Job Responsibilities: 1. Instructional Design & Pedagogical Consultation ●       Consult with faculty on the application of learning theories, instructional design models, and evidence-based teaching strategies to develop engaging, learner-centered business education courses ●       Guide instructors in aligning measurable learning outcomes with course activities, structural sequencing, and robust, authentic assessment methodologies ●       Consult with faculty on the ethical integration of Generative, Agentic, and other relevant AI tools (e.g., LLMs, specialized business simulators) into coursework, assisting them in developing clear classroom AI policies and innovative, \"AI-active\" or \"AI-resistant\" assessment models ●       Use AI-assisted workflows to accelerate course mapping, draft creative case-study scripts, and generate diverse practice question pools aligned with professional accreditation standards ●       Conduct systematic quality reviews of new and existing courses using recognized quality assurance rubrics (e.g., Quality Matters) ●       Ensure all instructional materials, course layouts, and digital media adhere strictly to federal, state, and institutional accessibility guidelines (ADA, Section 508, and Universal Design for Learning principles) 2. Multimedia Production & Technology Integration ●       Collaborate directly with the CoBE Tech media production team to coordinate the design and seamless integration of high-quality visual graphics, animations, podcast setups, and broadcast-quality video/audio components into course environments ●       Utilize course authoring tools and the institutional Learning Management System (LMS) to build interactive, visually engaging learning modules ●       Evaluate emerging instructional innovations, educational applications, and AI-driven workflow solutions, recommending strategic equipment or software implementation to the Assistant Dean ●       Provide advanced troubleshooting and technical consultation for instructional technology platforms utilized within the college 3. Faculty Training & Support ●       Develop, coordinate, and deliver comprehensive training programs, workshops, and documentation for faculty and staff regarding instructional design best practices and LMS functionality ●       Provide supportive, client-focused technical assistance to educators across a wide spectrum of technical comfort and experience levels ●       As operationally feasible and approved by leadership, this position may include opportunities to teach courses within the college 4. Strategic Alignment & Administrative Support ●       Represent CoBE Tech on campus-wide committees and cross-functional initiatives related to institutional teaching, learning, and digital learning strategies ●       Assist the Assistant Dean with regular technology needs assessments to inform future resource and equipment planning Department: College of Business and Economics Compensation: Well-qualified candidates can expect a starting annual salary within a range of $65,000 – $75,000. UW System employees receive an excellent benefit package. To learn more about the UW System’s comprehensive benefit package, review the UW System  Employee Benefits Brochure . Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in Instructional Design, Educational Technology, Education, or a closely related field Minimum of 2 years of professional experience designing and developing courses within an education environment across multiple modalities (online, hyflex, face-to-face) Demonstrated proficiency with enterprise-level Learning Management Systems (such as Canvas) Preferred Qualifications: Prior teaching or instructional facilitation experience in a Higher Education setting, with eligibility/desire to teach business-related courses Quality Matters (QM) Peer Reviewer certification Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Autonomy & Initiative: Strong ability to self-motivate, take initiative, prioritize projects, and work highly independently in a fast-paced environment Consultative Communication: Exceptional active listening, written, and oral communication skills, with a proven passion for assisting users of varying technical skill and comfort levels AI Literacy & Tool Fluency: Practical understanding of Generative AI platforms and prompt-engineering frameworks (e.g., ChatGPT, Claude, Microsoft Copilot) and how to apply them responsibly to course creation, writing assistance, and workflow automation Design Frameworks: Deep knowledge of adult learning principles, modern assessment strategies, and quality assurance frameworks Accessibility & Compliance: Thorough understanding of digital accessibility standards (WCAG 2.1, ADA, Section 508) and student data privacy regulations (FERPA) Collaborative Mindset: Strong ability to collaborate effectively with faculty, media production specialists, and campus IT partners to achieve unified project goals Ability to interact respectfully with people with diverse socioeconomic, cultural and ethnic backgrounds Willingness and ability to be an active participant in following applicable safety rules and regulations including necessary training and drills How to Apply: Applications received by  August 26, 2026  are ensured full consideration. The most qualified applicants will be invited to participate in the next step of the selection process. Only complete application packages will be considered. This includes online submission of the following documents: Cover Letter Resume Name and contact information for three professional references Contact Information: For questions regarding this position, please contact: Heather Taplick Dean Assistant 262-472-1343 taplickh@uww.edu If you have questions regarding this recruitment or if you are unable to complete the application online due to a disability or system problem, please contact us at (262) 472-1024 or  hr@uww.edu . CONDITIONS OF APPOINTMENT: University of Wisconsin-Whitewater does not offer H-1B or other work authorization visa sponsorship for this position. Candidates must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire and maintain work authorization throughout the employment term. If you have questions regarding this, please contact Human Resources.  UW-Whitewater is not an e-verify employer, therefore STEM extensions are not options for work authorization. CAMPUS INFORMATION: UW-Whitewater is a thriving public university located in southeastern Wisconsin. It leads the way in providing world-class, affordable higher education programs to more students than neighboring regional comprehensives in the University of Wisconsin System. A collaborative team of 1,300 faculty and staff are devoted to the success of the university’s 11,500 students at its main and Rock County campuses. The team provides high-impact practices and academic programs — online and in person — from associate to doctoral levels. Recognized nationally for affordability, inclusion, and career development, UWW serves approximately one-third first-generation students and boasts strength in many areas, especially business and education. The campus and community embrace the Warhawk Family spirit, supporting the university’s arts and nationally recognized NCAA Division-III level championship athletics. As a proud institution of access, more than 10 percent of undergraduate students use the acclaimed Center for Students with Disabilities — a priority mission since the 1970s.       UW-Whitewater's campuses are located centrally near Milwaukee, Madison, and Chicago, offering easy access to the cultural and commercial opportunities of major metropolitan areas. Near both Lake Geneva and the Wisconsin Dells, the area attracts numerous visitors from across the country. Whitewater is a vibrant college community near the Kettle Moraine State Forest offering area residents seasonal outdoor enjoyment. The nearby Rock County campus is located in Janesville, a town of 60,000 located on the Rock River and known as Wisconsin’s Park Place.   ORGANIZATION INFORMATION: The University of Wisconsin-Whitewater is part of the 13-campus Universities of Wisconsin ( https://www.wisconsin.edu ).    Wisconsin Statute 19.36(7)(b) provides that applicants may indicate in writing that their identity should be kept confidential. In response to a public records request, the University will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except that the identity of the successful final candidate will be released. See Wisconsin Statute 19.36(7)(a).    Per Regent Policy Document 20-19, University of Wisconsin System Criminal Background Check Policy and Universities of Wisconsin Administrative Policy 1275 Recruitment Policies, UW-Whitewater requires criminal background checks as a contingency to employment. A criminal background check will be conducted prior to an offer of employment. All final candidates must be asked, prior to hire, whether they have been found to have engaged in, are currently under investigation for, or left employment during an active investigation in which they were accused of sexual violence or sexual harassment. When obtaining employment reference checks, these same sexual violence or sexual harassment questions must also be asked.    The University of Wisconsin-Whitewater requires that all employees be active participants in following applicable safety rules and regulations including necessary training and drills.    For UW-Whitewater Campus safety information and crime statistics/annual Security Report, see  Annual Security and Fire Safety Report if you would like a paper copy of the report please contact the UW-Whitewater Police at 262-472-4660. UW IS AN EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
